# Docs and archive

When a **big change** lands, **docs are updated as part of the same change** and **whatever it retires is moved into `.archive/` and committed**. A big change is not finished until both are done.

Related: **`feature-ship.mdc`** (this is a ship gate), **`repo-hygiene.mdc`**, **`branch-commit-pr.mdc`**.

## What counts as a big change

Any of these:

- Hosting, deploy pipeline, domain or DNS changes.
- A data store, sync model, or the public API contract changes (`worker/API.md`, `/v1/*`).
- An app, package, service, or workflow is added, replaced, or retired.
- Policy changes (`mobile/docs/zero-cost-policy.md`, privacy, no-ads, donations).
- A change that makes an existing doc, diagram, or rule untrue.

Not big: bug fixes, copy tweaks, dependency bumps, refactors that keep behavior and docs true.

## Docs gate (same commit or same PR as the change)

Before calling the change done, walk this list and update every item that is now wrong or missing:

| Doc | When |
|-----|------|
| `README.md` | Setup, run, deploy, hosting, or architecture changed |
| `worker/API.md` | Any `/v1/*` or `/ingest` shape, header, or limit changed |
| `mobile/docs/*.md` | Cost, CORS, backup, or product-ethos facts changed |
| `.cursor/rules/*.mdc` | A rule describes something that no longer exists |
| Plan/architecture page (HTML + SVG) | A diagram or "today vs plan" claim changed |
| Sibling repo docs (e.g. tabocalypse `PRIVACY.md`) | The shared API or a disclosed host changed |

Rules for the docs themselves:

- State what is true now. No "will be" for things that already shipped, no "is" for things not built.
- Keep the migration note (the project was migrated from ai.studio) in the README header.
- Say "source of truth" for D1 and "replica" for browser copies. Never "diary" in technical docs.
- Do not claim things that were not verified (for example edge caching). Mark them unverified.
- List the docs you updated in the PR Summary, or say "no doc impact" and why.

## Archive gate (retire, do not delete)

Anything a big change **replaces or retires** moves to `.archive/` instead of being deleted, and the move is **committed in the same change**.

1. Pick a folder: `.archive/YYYY-MM-DD-<short-slug>/`.
2. Move with `git mv` so history follows the files. Keep the original relative paths inside the folder.
3. Add `ARCHIVED.md` in that folder: what it was, why it was retired, what replaced it, the last commit hash where it was live, and how to restore it.
4. Fix references to the old paths (docs, workflows, configs) so nothing still points at them.
5. Commit the archive together with the docs update. Do not leave it uncommitted or git-ignored.

Archive rules:

- `.archive/` is read-only history. Nothing in the live app, worker, build, or CI may import from it, run it, or deploy it.
- It is excluded from builds by design. Root tooling lists explicit folders, and `mobile/` only lints and tests itself. If a new tool globs the repo root, exclude `.archive/` there.
- Old docs get a banner on the first line: `> Archived YYYY-MM-DD. Superseded by <path or link>.`
- **Never archive secrets.** Remove them, rotate them, and say so in the PR. Credentials stay out of the repo.
- **Never archive generated output** (`node_modules/`, `dist/`, `coverage/`, build artifacts). `repo-hygiene.mdc` still deletes those.
- Do not archive trivial or still-used files. If it is still live, it is not archived.
- Old chat or plan artifacts that matter go here too, not only in someone's home folder.

## Ship checklist (add to the PR Test plan)

- [ ] Docs gate: updated, or "no doc impact" with a reason.
- [ ] Archive gate: retired items moved to `.archive/` with `ARCHIVED.md`, committed.
- [ ] No live file references an archived path.
